{
  "slug": "artist:dj-screw",
  "name": "DJ Screw",
  "editorial_article": "Born Robert Earl Davis Jr. in 1971, the Houston-based producer pioneered the chopped and screwed style, a technique characterized by slowing down records and manipulating samples to create a lethargic, syrupy aesthetic. He founded the influential collective known as the Screwed Up Click, which included prominent regional artists such as Big Moe, Fat Pat, and Lil' Keke. His 1995 compilation album '3 'n the Mornin' (Part Two), released on Bigtyme Recordz, serves as a seminal document of this sound, layering Dirty South rap vocals over his signature slow-motion production. Recorded at Samplified Digital in Houston, the project highlights his role as both a curator and remixer, incorporating guest appearances from artists like E.S.G. and the Botany Boyz. Before gaining widespread recognition, Screw acknowledged the influence of Texas DJ Darryl Scott on his approach to mixtape culture. His work remains synonymous with the identity of Houston hip hop, and he maintained a consistent creative output until his death in 2000.",
